ui-use-primary-tabs-when-only-one-set-of-tabs-is-needed-use-secondary-tabs-when-a-screen-need = Use primary tabs when only one set of tabs is needed. Use secondary tabs when a screen needs more than one level of hierarchy; they have a simpler indicator but the same function.

ui-tabs-can-be-joined-with-components-like-app-bars-embedded-in-a-specific-ui-region-or-neste = Tabs can be joined with components like app bars, embedded in a specific UI region, or nested within components like cards and sheets. Tabs control the UI region displayed below them.

ui-tabs-are-displayed-in-a-single-row-with-each-tab-connected-to-the-content-it-represents-as = Tabs are displayed in a single row, with each tab connected to the content it represents. As a set, all tabs are unified by a shared topic. Secondary tabs are always placed below primary tabs.

ui-secondary-tabs-are-found-within-other-content-to-assist-users-with-greater-detail = Secondary tabs are found within other content to assist users with greater detail

ui-the-maximum-width-of-fixed-tabs-is-set-by-the-widest-tab-fixed-tabs-use-a-fluid-margin-and = The maximum width of fixed tabs is set by the widest tab. Fixed tabs use a fluid margin and are aligned to the center or leading edge of the body region. Avoid more than four fixed tabs; with five or more, the container becomes cramped.

ui-tabs-can-grow-in-width-in-relation-to-the-number-of-items-contained-within = Tabs can grow in width in relation to the number of items contained within

